[{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles","text":"Documentation for DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Exported-Functionality","page":"Home","title":"Exported Functionality","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#Available-Ensembles","page":"Home","title":"Available Ensembles","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#Internal-Functions","page":"Home","title":"Internal Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.DiscretePolynomialEnsemble","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.DiscretePolynomialEnsemble","text":"DiscretePolynomialEnsemble <: PolynomialEnsemble\n\nAbstract type for discrete polynomial ensembles.\n\nInterface\n\nSubtypes must implement:\n\nensemble[n](x): evaluate basis polynomial of degree n at x\nweight(ensemble, x): weight function at x\nLinearAlgebra.norm_sqr(ensemble[n]): squared norm of basis element n\nfraction_leading_coefficients(ensemble, n): ratio of leading coefficients of degrees n and n-1\n\nOptionally implement transform(ensemble, x) for coordinate transformations (default: identity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Kernel","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Kernel","text":"Kernel(ensemble, n)\n\nA rank-n kernel for ensemble constructed from the first n basis elements, using the Christoffel-Darboux formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.weight","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.weight","text":"weight(ensemble, x)\n\nWeight function of ensemble at point x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.BesselJ","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.BesselJ","text":"BesselJ(; θ)\n\nBessel J ensemble with parameter θ > 0. Construct the Bessel kernel via Kernel(ensemble).\n\nwarning: Warning\nNot an actual polynomial ensemble. Basis elements are Bessel functions, not polynomials.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Charlier","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Charlier","text":"Charlier(; a)\n\nCharlier polynomial ensemble with parameter a > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.DiscreteLegendre","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.DiscreteLegendre","text":"DiscreteLegendre(; N)\n\nDiscrete Legendre polynomial ensemble on {0, 1, ..., N}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Hahn","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Hahn","text":"Hahn(; α, β, M)\n\nHahn polynomial ensemble with parameters α > -1, β > -1, and integer M.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Krawtchouk","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Krawtchouk","text":"Krawtchouk(; K, p)\n\nKrawtchouk polynomial ensemble with parameters K (number of trials) and 0 < p < 1 (success probability).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Lanczos","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Lanczos","text":"Lanczos(w, domain; ν = identity, simplify = identity)\n\nOrthonormal polynomial ensemble via Lanczos algorithm with weight function w on domain. If a transformation ν is specified, the inner product used for orthonormalization is f g = _x  𝒟 w(x) f(ν(x)) g(ν(x)) and the polynomials will be over ν(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.LanczosMonic","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.LanczosMonic","text":"LanczosMonic(w, domain; ν = identity, simplify = identity)\n\nMonic polynomial ensemble via Lanczos algorithm with weight function w on domain. If a transformation ν is specified, the inner product used for orthogonalization is f g = _x  𝒟 w(x) f(ν(x)) g(ν(x)) and the polynomials will be over ν(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Meixner","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.Meixner","text":"Meixner(; K, q)\n\nMeixner polynomial ensemble with parameters K > 0 and 0 < q < 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.dJdν","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.dJdν","text":"dJdν(ν, z, precision = 1.0e-14, max_steps = 10^6; prec)\n\nCompute the derivative of the Bessel function J_ν(z) with respect to ν.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.digamma_over_gamma-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.digamma_over_gamma","text":"digamma_over_gamma(n)\n\nCompute digamma(n)/gamma(n), handling the case when n is a non-positive integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.fraction_leading_coefficients","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.fraction_leading_coefficients","text":"fraction_leading_coefficients(ensemble, n)\n\nRatio of leading coefficients κₙ₋₁/κₙ for basis polynomials of degrees n and n-1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.grad_pfq","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.grad_pfq","text":"grad_pFq(pfq_val, a, b, z, precision = 1.0e-14, max_steps = 10^6; prec)\n\nReturns the gradient of the generalized hypergeometric function wrt to the input arguments:\n\n    _pF_q(a_1a_pb_1b_qz)\n\nWhere:\n\n    fracpartial partial a_1 =\n     sum_k=1^infty\n       frac\n         left(1 + sum_m=0^k-1frac1m+a_1right)\n           * left(prod_j=1^pleft(a_jright)_kright)z^k\n         kprod_j=1^qleft(b_jright)_k\n       - _pF_q(a_1a_pb_1b_qz)\n\n    fracpartial partial b_1 =\n     _pF_q(a_1a_pb_1b_qz) -\n     sum_k=1^infty\n       frac\n         left(1 + sum_m=0^k-1frac1m+b_1right)\n           * left(prod_j=1^pleft(a_jright)_kright)z^k\n         kprod_j=1^qleft(b_jright)_k\n\n    fracpartial partial z =\n    fracprod_j=1^p(a_j)prod_j=1^q (b_j)\n    _pF_q(a_1+1a_p+1b_1+1b_q+1z)\n\nNoting the the recurrence relation for the digamma function: psi(x + 1) = psi(x) + frac1x, the gradients for the function with respect to a and b then simplify to:\n\n    fracpartial partial a_1 =\n     sum_k=1^infty\n       frac\n         left(1 + sum_m=0^k-1frac1m+a_1right)\n           * left(prod_j=1^pleft(a_jright)_kright)z^k\n         kprod_j=1^qleft(b_jright)_k\n       - _pF_q(a_1a_pb_1b_qz)\n\n    fracpartial partial b_1 =\n     _pF_q(a_1a_pb_1b_qz) -\n     sum_k=1^infty\n       frac\n         left(1 + sum_m=0^k-1frac1m+b_1right)\n           * left(prod_j=1^pleft(a_jright)_kright)z^k\n         kprod_j=1^qleft(b_jright)_k\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"#DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.transform-Tuple{DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.PolynomialEnsemble, Any}","page":"Home","title":"DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.transform","text":"transform(ensemble, x)\n\nCoordinate transformation applied before polynomial evaluation. Default: identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#LinearAlgebra.norm_sqr-Tuple{DiscretePolynomialEnsembles.BasisElement}","page":"Home","title":"LinearAlgebra.norm_sqr","text":"norm_sqr(ensemble[n])\n\nSquared L² norm of basis element n with respect to the ensemble weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"}]
